# kids.shapes as a matrix: the shapes, the families and what follows from them. The facts are the knowledge base (kb.sop) and what the dialogue teaches; the
# words of the answers stay in the world (templates, matrix.templates.sop and fns.mjs).
#
# Left to the operations (ops.mjs, `ops=` in world.sop), because a library query cannot say them: the checks that carry the value they seek ("is a banana
# red?", "is red a primary color?": the library would answer the value it was given), the colors of a thing (several colors are all true, not a conflict)
# and what two colors make or which colors make one (the facts are read in both orders).

# ---- what a shape is, from its sides and corners: round when it has no sides and no corners, flat when it has any (or when all that is known is a count)
@sh.s0 kids.shapes.shape sides=0 corners=0
@sh.round kids.shapes.shape core.on=$sh.s0 summary=round
@sh.r.round core.rule when=$sh.s0 then=$sh.round

@sh.s1 kids.shapes.shape sides=?n
@sh.t.sides core.check fn=gt args=?n args=0
@sh.flat1 kids.shapes.shape core.on=$sh.s1 summary=flat
@sh.r.flat1 core.rule when=$sh.s1 if=$sh.t.sides then=$sh.flat1

@sh.s2 kids.shapes.shape corners=?n
@sh.t.corners core.check fn=gt args=?n args=0
@sh.flat2 kids.shapes.shape core.on=$sh.s2 summary=flat
@sh.r.flat2 core.rule when=$sh.s2 if=$sh.t.corners then=$sh.flat2

@sh.s3 kids.shapes.shape sides=0 core.absent=corners
@sh.flat3 kids.shapes.shape core.on=$sh.s3 summary=flat
@sh.r.flat3 core.rule when=$sh.s3 then=$sh.flat3

@sh.s4 kids.shapes.shape corners=0 core.absent=sides
@sh.flat4 kids.shapes.shape core.on=$sh.s4 summary=flat
@sh.r.flat4 core.rule when=$sh.s4 then=$sh.flat4
